WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.486
Input validation can reduce the likelihood and impact of common vulnerabilities, but it should be paired with safer design patterns.

00:00:09.486 --> 00:00:18.090
Injection risks occur when untrusted data is interpreted as part of a command, query, expression, template, or protocol.

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:27.135
Validation can reject unexpected input, but safe APIs such as parameterized queries and structured command invocation are still required.

00:00:27.135 --> 00:00:35.298
Path traversal and unsafe file upload issues often involve weak assumptions about names, paths, extensions, or content.

00:00:35.298 --> 00:00:41.254
Validation should define acceptable filenames, storage locations, file sizes, and content expectations.

00:00:41.254 --> 00:00:44.563
Normalization should happen before path decisions.

00:00:44.563 --> 00:00:51.843
The application should still enforce access controls and avoid serving uploaded content in unsafe ways.

00:00:51.843 --> 00:00:58.682
Broken business logic and mass assignment show that validation is not only about characters.

00:00:58.682 --> 00:01:13.463
A request may be perfectly formatted but still unacceptable because it changes a field the user should not control, skips a workflow step, or violates a relationship between account, tenant, and resource.

00:01:13.463 --> 00:01:20.302
Validation should include business rules, and authorization should enforce what each user can do.

00:01:20.302 --> 00:01:27.582
Some risks, such as request smuggling and unsafe deserialization, involve parsing boundaries and high-level architecture.

00:01:27.582 --> 00:01:33.980
Validation can help reject unexpected structures, but it is not a complete fix.

00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:42.142
Use well-maintained frameworks, consistent parsing behavior, safe serialization formats, output encoding, secure defaults, and defense in depth.

00:01:42.142 --> 00:01:47.658
Validation is most effective when it supports a broader secure design.
